1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,236
Sweetheart, if you came to my house

2
00:00:02,236 --> 00:00:04,729
any December since nineteen sixty-five,

3
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:06,582
you left with a little tin of

4
00:00:06,582 --> 00:00:06,965
fudge.

5
00:00:07,145 --> 00:00:09,376
Every neighbor, every teacher,

6
00:00:09,376 --> 00:00:11,161
the mailman, the pastor,

7
00:00:11,161 --> 00:00:13,392
the boy who shoveled the walk.

8
00:00:13,572 --> 00:00:16,300
One year I made fourteen batches.

9
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,297
And people still stop me at church

10
00:00:19,297 --> 00:00:20,457
and ask, Ruth,

11
00:00:20,457 --> 00:00:23,274
how does yours come out so smooth,

12
00:00:23,274 --> 00:00:26,174
when mine turns to sand every time?

13
00:00:26,524 --> 00:00:28,535
So tonight I'm going to give you

14
00:00:28,535 --> 00:00:29,729
my Christmas fudge.

15
00:00:29,909 --> 00:00:32,259
Every ingredient, every step,

16
00:00:32,259 --> 00:00:35,338
and the three mistakes that turn fudge

17
00:00:35,338 --> 00:00:37,850
grainy, so you never make them.

18
00:00:38,030 --> 00:00:40,861
It takes about fifteen minutes and one

19
00:00:40,861 --> 00:00:43,096
pot, and it makes a whole nine

20
00:00:43,096 --> 00:00:44,288
by thirteen pan.

21
00:00:44,468 --> 00:00:45,664
Get your pencil,

22
00:00:45,664 --> 00:00:48,130
honey, and maybe a glass of milk,

23
00:00:48,130 --> 00:00:50,896
because you're going to want to taste

24
00:00:50,896 --> 00:00:51,120
it.

25
00:00:51,470 --> 00:00:52,744
When I was a girl,

26
00:00:52,744 --> 00:00:55,151
my mother made fudge the hard way.

27
00:00:55,331 --> 00:00:58,181
Sugar, milk, cocoa and butter in her

28
00:00:58,181 --> 00:01:00,793
heavy pot, boiled until a drop in

29
00:01:00,793 --> 00:01:03,010
a glass of cold water made a

30
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:05,385
soft ball between her fingers.

31
00:01:05,565 --> 00:01:07,785
Then she beat it with a wooden

32
00:01:07,785 --> 00:01:09,710
spoon until her arm ached.

33
00:01:09,890 --> 00:01:11,736
Some years it was perfect.

34
00:01:11,916 --> 00:01:13,940
Some years it was like gravel,

35
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:16,031
and my father ate it anyway and

36
00:01:16,031 --> 00:01:17,987
said it was the best she ever

37
00:01:17,987 --> 00:01:18,325
made.

38
00:01:18,674 --> 00:01:20,728
Then in the nineteen sixties,

39
00:01:20,728 --> 00:01:23,135
a recipe started showing up on the

40
00:01:23,135 --> 00:01:25,826
back of the jars of marshmallow creme.

41
00:01:26,006 --> 00:01:28,386
People called it fantasy fudge.

42
00:01:28,566 --> 00:01:31,639
My mother tried it in nineteen sixty-four,

43
00:01:31,639 --> 00:01:33,834
the Christmas I turned twenty,

44
00:01:33,834 --> 00:01:35,590
and she never went back.

45
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:39,254
The marshmallow keeps the sugar from making

46
00:01:39,254 --> 00:01:42,414
crystals, so it comes out smooth almost

47
00:01:42,414 --> 00:01:43,305
every time.

48
00:01:43,485 --> 00:01:45,320
That's the one I'm giving you.

49
00:01:45,669 --> 00:01:47,791
The first year I made it on

50
00:01:47,791 --> 00:01:50,228
my own was nineteen sixty-five,

51
00:01:50,228 --> 00:01:53,136
in my first little kitchen on Diamond

52
00:01:53,136 --> 00:01:55,572
Street, on a stove that ran hot

53
00:01:55,572 --> 00:01:56,908
on the left side.

54
00:01:57,088 --> 00:01:59,549
I burned the first batch black.

55
00:01:59,729 --> 00:02:02,167
The second batch I stood there with

56
00:02:02,167 --> 00:02:04,048
my eyes on the clock like a

57
00:02:04,048 --> 00:02:06,208
hawk, and it came out so smooth

58
00:02:06,208 --> 00:02:08,367
my husband ate a quarter of the

59
00:02:08,367 --> 00:02:09,621
pan before supper.

60
00:02:09,801 --> 00:02:12,299
That's when I learned the whole secret

61
00:02:12,299 --> 00:02:14,073
of this fudge is the clock.

62
00:02:14,423 --> 00:02:16,139
Here's what you need,

63
00:02:16,139 --> 00:02:16,629
honey.

64
00:02:16,809 --> 00:02:18,864
Three cups of white sugar.

65
00:02:19,044 --> 00:02:21,393
Three quarters of a cup of butter,

66
00:02:21,393 --> 00:02:23,189
that's a stick and a half.

67
00:02:23,369 --> 00:02:25,848
Two thirds of a cup of evaporated

68
00:02:25,848 --> 00:02:27,275
milk, from the can,

69
00:02:27,275 --> 00:02:29,453
not sweetened condensed milk.

70
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:32,111
Remember that, they look alike on the

71
00:02:32,111 --> 00:02:34,188
shelf and they are not the same

72
00:02:34,188 --> 00:02:34,590
thing.

73
00:02:34,770 --> 00:02:38,295
One twelve ounce bag of semi-sweet chocolate

74
00:02:38,295 --> 00:02:38,776
chips.

75
00:02:38,956 --> 00:02:42,102
One seven ounce jar of marshmallow creme.

76
00:02:42,282 --> 00:02:44,662
And one teaspoon of vanilla.

77
00:02:45,011 --> 00:02:47,554
For tools, you need a heavy three

78
00:02:47,554 --> 00:02:49,943
quart saucepan, a wooden spoon,

79
00:02:49,943 --> 00:02:52,024
and a nine by thirteen pan.

80
00:02:52,204 --> 00:02:54,687
Line the pan with foil or parchment,

81
00:02:54,687 --> 00:02:57,239
with the edges hanging over the sides

82
00:02:57,239 --> 00:02:59,170
like handles, and butter it.

83
00:02:59,350 --> 00:03:01,366
That way you lift the whole block

84
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:02,222
out to cut it,

85
00:03:02,222 --> 00:03:03,871
and you don't dig it out of

86
00:03:03,871 --> 00:03:05,399
the corners with a knife.

87
00:03:05,579 --> 00:03:08,632
Grandma Pearl's pans had knife scratches all

88
00:03:08,632 --> 00:03:11,060
over the bottom from fifty years of

89
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:11,477
fudge.

90
00:03:11,826 --> 00:03:14,021
And here's my first secret,

91
00:03:14,021 --> 00:03:14,915
sweetheart.

92
00:03:15,095 --> 00:03:18,287
Measure everything and open everything before you

93
00:03:18,287 --> 00:03:19,460
turn on the stove.

94
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,687
Open the jar of marshmallow,

95
00:03:21,687 --> 00:03:23,515
open the chocolate chips,

96
00:03:23,515 --> 00:03:26,147
have the vanilla on the counter with

97
00:03:26,147 --> 00:03:27,024
the cap off.

98
00:03:27,204 --> 00:03:28,801
Once that sugar boils,

99
00:03:28,801 --> 00:03:31,124
you can't walk away for a single

100
00:03:31,124 --> 00:03:33,664
second, and you can't be fighting a

101
00:03:33,664 --> 00:03:35,261
jar lid with one hand.

102
00:03:35,611 --> 00:03:36,633
Step one.

103
00:03:36,813 --> 00:03:37,838
Put the sugar,

104
00:03:37,838 --> 00:03:40,546
the butter and the evaporated milk in

105
00:03:40,546 --> 00:03:42,816
the saucepan, over medium heat.

106
00:03:42,996 --> 00:03:44,836
Stir it all the time with the

107
00:03:44,836 --> 00:03:47,311
wooden spoon until the butter melts and

108
00:03:47,311 --> 00:03:48,580
the sugar dissolves.

109
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:51,131
Rub a little between your fingers if

110
00:03:51,131 --> 00:03:52,185
you're not sure.

111
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:54,150
If it feels gritty,

112
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:55,465
keep stirring.

113
00:03:55,645 --> 00:03:58,688
That's important, because a grain of sugar

114
00:03:58,688 --> 00:04:01,659
that doesn't melt now becomes sand later.

115
00:04:02,008 --> 00:04:03,030
Step two.

116
00:04:03,210 --> 00:04:05,660
Bring it to a full rolling boil.

117
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:08,410
That means a boil you can't stir

118
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:08,812
down.

119
00:04:08,992 --> 00:04:10,972
Big bubbles all over,

120
00:04:10,972 --> 00:04:12,858
even while you stir.

121
00:04:13,038 --> 00:04:14,652
Now look at the clock.

122
00:04:14,832 --> 00:04:16,571
Boil it for four minutes,

123
00:04:16,571 --> 00:04:18,101
stirring all the time,

124
00:04:18,101 --> 00:04:20,118
all the way to the bottom and

125
00:04:20,118 --> 00:04:22,762
into the corners so it doesn't scorch.

126
00:04:22,942 --> 00:04:24,920
If you have a candy thermometer,

127
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:27,702
you want two hundred and thirty-four degrees.

128
00:04:27,882 --> 00:04:28,817
If you don't,

129
00:04:28,817 --> 00:04:31,121
four minutes at a full boil gets

130
00:04:31,121 --> 00:04:31,841
you there.

131
00:04:32,190 --> 00:04:33,410
Step three.

132
00:04:33,590 --> 00:04:34,971
Take it off the heat.

133
00:04:35,151 --> 00:04:37,435
Right away, dump in the whole bag

134
00:04:37,435 --> 00:04:40,066
of chocolate chips and stir until they

135
00:04:40,066 --> 00:04:42,419
melt and it's all one shiny brown.

136
00:04:42,599 --> 00:04:45,138
Then add the marshmallow creme and the

137
00:04:45,138 --> 00:04:47,677
vanilla, and stir hard until there are

138
00:04:47,677 --> 00:04:49,147
no white streaks left.

139
00:04:49,327 --> 00:04:51,832
Your arm will get a little tired,

140
00:04:51,832 --> 00:04:52,288
honey.

141
00:04:52,468 --> 00:04:54,658
That's how you know you're doing it

142
00:04:54,658 --> 00:04:55,033
right.

143
00:04:55,383 --> 00:04:56,777
Step four.

144
00:04:56,957 --> 00:04:59,146
Pour it into your pan right away,

145
00:04:59,146 --> 00:05:01,203
and smooth it out with the back

146
00:05:01,203 --> 00:05:02,065
of the spoon.

147
00:05:02,245 --> 00:05:05,001
Work fast, because it starts to set

148
00:05:05,001 --> 00:05:06,576
as soon as it cools.

149
00:05:06,756 --> 00:05:09,379
Then leave it alone on the counter,

150
00:05:09,379 --> 00:05:10,878
at room temperature,

151
00:05:10,878 --> 00:05:12,677
for at least four hours,

152
00:05:12,677 --> 00:05:13,652
or overnight.

153
00:05:13,832 --> 00:05:15,958
Don't put it in the icebox to

154
00:05:15,958 --> 00:05:16,618
hurry it.

155
00:05:16,798 --> 00:05:18,760
It sets better slow.

156
00:05:19,110 --> 00:05:21,134
How do you know it's right,

157
00:05:21,134 --> 00:05:21,583
honey?

158
00:05:21,763 --> 00:05:23,681
When you stir it in the pan,

159
00:05:23,681 --> 00:05:25,187
it should look glossy,

160
00:05:25,187 --> 00:05:26,215
like wet paint,

161
00:05:26,215 --> 00:05:28,611
and the spoon should leave a little

162
00:05:28,611 --> 00:05:30,529
trail that slowly closes up.

163
00:05:30,709 --> 00:05:32,976
If it's already stiff and dull in

164
00:05:32,976 --> 00:05:35,106
the pot, you boiled it a little

165
00:05:35,106 --> 00:05:35,724
too long.

166
00:05:35,904 --> 00:05:38,561
Work fast and it will still be

167
00:05:38,561 --> 00:05:39,004
good.

168
00:05:39,184 --> 00:05:41,787
If it's thin and runs like syrup,

169
00:05:41,787 --> 00:05:43,759
it needed another minute.

170
00:05:43,939 --> 00:05:46,203
Next batch, you'll know.

171
00:05:46,552 --> 00:05:48,401
Now the three mistakes,

172
00:05:48,401 --> 00:05:51,938
sweetheart, because this is where most fudge

173
00:05:51,938 --> 00:05:52,822
goes wrong.

174
00:05:53,002 --> 00:05:54,302
Mistake one.

175
00:05:54,482 --> 00:05:56,572
Not boiling it long enough.

176
00:05:56,752 --> 00:05:58,522
If you take it off at two

177
00:05:58,522 --> 00:06:00,788
minutes because you got nervous,

178
00:06:00,788 --> 00:06:01,779
it never sets.

179
00:06:01,959 --> 00:06:04,037
You'll have chocolate sauce.

180
00:06:04,217 --> 00:06:06,159
Delicious on ice cream,

181
00:06:06,159 --> 00:06:07,340
but not fudge.

182
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:09,775
Use a timer and trust the four

183
00:06:09,775 --> 00:06:10,376
minutes.

184
00:06:10,726 --> 00:06:12,201
Mistake two.

185
00:06:12,381 --> 00:06:14,759
Scraping the sides of the pot when

186
00:06:14,759 --> 00:06:15,388
you pour.

187
00:06:15,568 --> 00:06:17,600
Up on the sides of the pot,

188
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:19,557
sugar crystals have dried,

189
00:06:19,557 --> 00:06:21,891
and if you scrape them into the

190
00:06:21,891 --> 00:06:24,751
pan, they turn the whole batch grainy.

191
00:06:24,931 --> 00:06:27,140
Pour, and let what stays in the

192
00:06:27,140 --> 00:06:28,565
pot stay in the pot.

193
00:06:28,745 --> 00:06:30,594
That's the cook's treat,

194
00:06:30,594 --> 00:06:31,056
honey.

195
00:06:31,236 --> 00:06:33,111
I always gave the pot and the

196
00:06:33,111 --> 00:06:35,439
spoon to whoever was in the kitchen.

197
00:06:35,788 --> 00:06:37,217
Mistake three.

198
00:06:37,397 --> 00:06:39,962
Using the wrong milk or cheap chocolate.

199
00:06:40,142 --> 00:06:43,071
Sweetened condensed milk makes it too sweet

200
00:06:43,071 --> 00:06:45,250
and it won't set right with this

201
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:45,727
recipe.

202
00:06:45,907 --> 00:06:49,028
And the cheapest store chocolate chips have

203
00:06:49,028 --> 00:06:49,899
wax in them,

204
00:06:49,899 --> 00:06:52,367
so the fudge looks dull and tastes

205
00:06:52,367 --> 00:06:53,384
like a candle.

206
00:06:53,564 --> 00:06:55,920
Buy good semi-sweet chips.

207
00:06:56,100 --> 00:06:58,385
It's the only thing in this recipe

208
00:06:58,385 --> 00:07:00,535
worth spending a little more on.

209
00:07:00,885 --> 00:07:02,133
When it's firm,

210
00:07:02,133 --> 00:07:04,710
lift it out by the foil handles

211
00:07:04,710 --> 00:07:06,540
and peel the foil off.

212
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:08,757
Cut it with a big sharp knife

213
00:07:08,757 --> 00:07:11,074
dipped in hot water and wiped dry

214
00:07:11,074 --> 00:07:12,339
between every cut.

215
00:07:12,519 --> 00:07:14,212
That gives you clean,

216
00:07:14,212 --> 00:07:16,791
square edges like a candy store.

217
00:07:16,971 --> 00:07:19,328
Small squares, about an inch.

218
00:07:19,508 --> 00:07:20,947
Fudge is rich,

219
00:07:20,947 --> 00:07:21,563
honey.

220
00:07:21,743 --> 00:07:24,088
Nobody needs a piece the size of

221
00:07:24,088 --> 00:07:26,799
a brick, no matter what your grandson

222
00:07:26,799 --> 00:07:27,165
says.

223
00:07:27,514 --> 00:07:28,272
To keep it,

224
00:07:28,272 --> 00:07:30,405
layer the squares in a tin with

225
00:07:30,405 --> 00:07:32,470
wax paper between every layer,

226
00:07:32,470 --> 00:07:34,260
and keep the lid on tight.

227
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:37,298
It stays good at room temperature for

228
00:07:37,298 --> 00:07:38,534
about two weeks,

229
00:07:38,534 --> 00:07:40,233
if it lasts that long.

230
00:07:40,413 --> 00:07:42,413
And it freezes beautifully,

231
00:07:42,413 --> 00:07:45,301
wrapped tight, for two or three months.

232
00:07:45,481 --> 00:07:48,085
I start my Christmas fudge right after

233
00:07:48,085 --> 00:07:50,483
Thanksgiving and freeze half of it,

234
00:07:50,483 --> 00:07:53,155
so I'm not cooking on the twenty-third.

235
00:07:53,505 --> 00:07:56,118
And a little warning from experience.

236
00:07:56,298 --> 00:07:59,421
Don't double this recipe in one pot.

237
00:07:59,601 --> 00:08:00,687
It sounds smart,

238
00:08:00,687 --> 00:08:03,267
but a double batch never boils evenly,

239
00:08:03,267 --> 00:08:05,778
the bottom scorches before the top is

240
00:08:05,778 --> 00:08:07,883
ready, and you end up with half

241
00:08:07,883 --> 00:08:09,512
a pan of fudge and a pot

242
00:08:09,512 --> 00:08:12,023
you'll be scrubbing until New Year's.

243
00:08:12,203 --> 00:08:13,552
If you need more,

244
00:08:13,552 --> 00:08:14,901
make two batches,

245
00:08:14,901 --> 00:08:16,488
one after the other.

246
00:08:16,668 --> 00:08:18,029
That's what I did,

247
00:08:18,029 --> 00:08:19,617
all fourteen of them.

248
00:08:19,966 --> 00:08:22,155
And if your kitchen is humid,

249
00:08:22,155 --> 00:08:24,193
honey, or it's a rainy day,

250
00:08:24,193 --> 00:08:26,456
boil it thirty seconds longer.

251
00:08:26,636 --> 00:08:29,063
Sugar candy hates damp weather.

252
00:08:29,243 --> 00:08:31,387
My mother wouldn't make fudge on a

253
00:08:31,387 --> 00:08:32,459
rainy day at all.

254
00:08:32,639 --> 00:08:34,814
She'd look out the window in the

255
00:08:34,814 --> 00:08:35,901
morning and say,

256
00:08:35,901 --> 00:08:38,688
not today, and we'd make cookies instead.

257
00:08:39,038 --> 00:08:41,153
Now a few ways to change it,

258
00:08:41,153 --> 00:08:43,496
once you've made the plain one.

259
00:08:43,676 --> 00:08:46,035
Stir in a cup of chopped walnuts

260
00:08:46,035 --> 00:08:46,846
at the end,

261
00:08:46,846 --> 00:08:49,353
that's how Grandma Pearl liked it.

262
00:08:49,533 --> 00:08:52,501
Or swap the vanilla for peppermint extract

263
00:08:52,501 --> 00:08:55,258
and sprinkle crushed candy canes on top

264
00:08:55,258 --> 00:08:56,812
while it's still soft.

265
00:08:56,992 --> 00:09:00,261
My grandchildren call that one the North

266
00:09:00,261 --> 00:09:01,160
Pole fudge.

267
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:03,930
Or use peanut butter chips instead of

268
00:09:03,930 --> 00:09:06,730
chocolate for a peanut butter fudge that

269
00:09:06,730 --> 00:09:08,690
disappears in one afternoon.

270
00:09:09,039 --> 00:09:10,245
And for gifts,

271
00:09:10,245 --> 00:09:13,602
sweetheart, you don't need fancy boxes.

272
00:09:13,782 --> 00:09:16,017
A clean jar with a square of

273
00:09:16,017 --> 00:09:18,252
red cloth tied over the lid.

274
00:09:18,432 --> 00:09:21,026
An old cookie tin from the thrift

275
00:09:21,026 --> 00:09:21,497
store.

276
00:09:21,677 --> 00:09:24,398
A paper plate covered in plastic wrap

277
00:09:24,398 --> 00:09:26,677
with a ribbon and a little card

278
00:09:26,677 --> 00:09:29,177
that says, made with love by Ruth.

279
00:09:29,357 --> 00:09:31,389
People don't remember the box.

280
00:09:31,569 --> 00:09:33,322
They remember that you made it.

281
00:09:33,672 --> 00:09:35,843
So here it is one more time.

282
00:09:36,023 --> 00:09:39,604
Sugar, butter and evaporated milk until there's

283
00:09:39,604 --> 00:09:40,214
no grit.

284
00:09:40,394 --> 00:09:43,250
A full rolling boil for four minutes.

285
00:09:43,430 --> 00:09:46,733
Chocolate, then marshmallow and vanilla,

286
00:09:46,733 --> 00:09:47,807
off the heat.

287
00:09:47,987 --> 00:09:49,647
Pour, don't scrape.

288
00:09:49,827 --> 00:09:51,522
Let it set slow.

289
00:09:51,702 --> 00:09:53,246
Cut with a hot knife.

290
00:09:53,426 --> 00:09:55,261
That's it, honey.

291
00:09:55,441 --> 00:09:58,109
Fifteen minutes, and you'll be the person

292
00:09:58,109 --> 00:10:00,843
everybody hopes is coming to the Christmas

293
00:10:00,843 --> 00:10:01,234
party.

294
00:10:01,584 --> 00:10:03,359
Tell me in the comments,

295
00:10:03,359 --> 00:10:04,173
sweetheart.

296
00:10:04,353 --> 00:10:06,487
Do you like your fudge plain,

297
00:10:06,487 --> 00:10:08,915
with walnuts, or with peppermint?

298
00:10:09,095 --> 00:10:12,079
And what recipe should I make next?

299
00:10:12,259 --> 00:10:14,720
Every day I'm bringing you one recipe

300
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:15,784
from my kitchen,

301
00:10:15,784 --> 00:10:18,112
the way my mother and Grandma Pearl

302
00:10:18,112 --> 00:10:18,645
made it.

303
00:10:18,825 --> 00:10:20,311
Good night, honey.

304
00:10:20,491 --> 00:10:22,232
Go make some fudge.
